#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158355} :activity/id #uuid "7117d1d2-5836-549a-9885-24bb3251be4d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048628 :topic/id #uuid "afd039a1-cbf1-5601-a4b7-0b332dbb15e4" :topic/math-academy-id 2926} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158438 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to *find the roots* of a function using *Newton’s method*. That is, we solved equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0,\n$$\n\nby starting with an initial guess and refining it step by step using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we turn to a closely related problem of *finding a local minimum or maximum* of a function $f(x)$. Recall that these may occur at points where the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to zero. In other words, we are looking for the roots of the function's derivative.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the function by a straight line near our current guess. Here, instead of using a linear approximation, we assume that the function $f(x)$ behaves like a *parabola* near our current guess. That’s a reasonable assumption if $f$ is smooth. \n\nTo make this idea precise, we use a second-order Taylor approximation, $F(x){:}$\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\dfrac{1}{2} f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum or maximum, we first need to take the derivative of this approximation, which is\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n).\n$$\n\nWe then need to set $F'(x) =0$:\n\n$$\nf'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}({x}_{n})(x-{x}_{n}) & =-{f}^{′}({x}_{n}) \\\\ x-{x}_{n} & =-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, just like in root-finding, we start with an initial guess and improve it step by step. But this time, we apply Newton’s method to the derivative $f'(x)$ instead of the function $f(x)$ itself:\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The second derivative $f''(x_n)$ must not be zero. If it is zero, or even very close to zero, the update step can become extremely large, leading us far away from the solution.\n\nJust like in root-finding, Newton’s method works best when the function is smooth, and our initial guess is already close to the minimum or maximum." :tutorial/id #uuid "0745939b-5be0-5c57-bc01-4f0f87024707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10738 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ba6dbaa2-2df5-5b25-ad1e-3045a5009cb6" :step/math-academy-id 688822 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158457 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nin the interval $[0,2].$ Let's calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.8.$ \n\nThe Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{2}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{4}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-x}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{{x}_{n}-1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}}}{\\frac{3-{x}_{n}}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(0.8)^{2}-1}{3-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.964,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "8b8920e9-7fa3-56ff-ab8e-2d0283bc3911" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13141 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b9795715-d19d-577f-99a4-49bef78578e8" :step/math-academy-id 688823 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157044 :question/id #uuid "35283781-7e25-5144-9bdd-77921cf5ea26" :question/math-academy-id "e-16703"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050483}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512925}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"} :step/id #uuid "2a82e30f-f0dc-525a-9d7f-def00a948b7a" :step/math-academy-id 688824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157045 :question/id #uuid "1bf35491-4dda-5bff-9a9e-c3e91f9d5edc" :question/math-academy-id "e-16704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557309}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"} :step/id #uuid "39b82888-3561-5f52-b687-f896a063cc7a" :step/math-academy-id 688827 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157046 :question/id #uuid "67b4f920-532d-56dc-8b6b-c76d5115959d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557251}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"} :step/id #uuid "c7207f91-013e-5d36-b69d-1544259c0ba7" :step/math-academy-id 688830}] :activity/title "Newton's Method for Optimizing Single-Variable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157519}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557406} :activity/id #uuid "d6344c23-c208-5a49-a584-a569b2952ea7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048628 :topic/id #uuid "afd039a1-cbf1-5601-a4b7-0b332dbb15e4" :topic/math-academy-id 2926} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.902" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556619} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d10b84c-ddb7-53d8-af30-74088c715ba6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.879" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556767} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b875e44-dea9-5add-bcd9-2d829e35888d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" :question/math-academy-id "q-256858" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[0.5,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})^{′}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2{x}_{n})}{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2)}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1} \\\\  & =0.878\\,555…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})+1} \\\\  & =(0.878\\,555…)-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+0.878\\,555…}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+1} \\\\  & =0.901\\,873…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈0.879$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.902,$ rounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "12de3978-4355-514f-ab7e-268d3e2d89e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57464dc7-bc2e-5b9c-aecb-331f510dce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556630} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac44f6e-0104-5745-83a5-1440a199990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈1.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556679} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556682} {:answer/id #uuid "a83a0cc8-6739-57e1-83af-4f3206bf0afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556847} {:answer/id #uuid "c08ae5fa-bc80-5a70-8390-cd92ea33a585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈1.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556682} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ec7d6b-6a31-5769-9cf5-7523c10eb966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-394147" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-12x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-12x)^{′}=6{x}^{5}-12, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-12)^{′}=30{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4})-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-12)}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{24{x}_{0}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.5)^{5}+12}{30(1.5)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.279\\,012…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{24{x}_{1}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.279\\,012…)^{5}+12}{30(1.279\\,012…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.172\\,682…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{24{x}_{2}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.172\\,682…)^{5}+12}{30(1.172\\,682…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.149\\,659…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{24{x}_{3}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{3}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.149\\,659…)^{5}+12}{30(1.149\\,659…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148\\,699….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.15$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "22962013-5da4-5a8a-ae59-f0ec55f33f51" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00608277-e348-507e-8515-26d9d8435c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈2.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556426} {:answer/id #uuid "190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈2.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556483} {:answer/id #uuid "95bbc949-7307-5b19-94e5-df2c13bf0a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈3.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556790} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2ea8de-39f8-5969-9d65-3f5ca48f745d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈2.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556885} {:answer/id #uuid "c162ef2f-cc9b-56e9-8583-03470ec95cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x≈2.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556483} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed233c25-8953-5cf6-8918-dc5828016abb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-394148" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5$ has a critical point in the interval $[2,4],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=3.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5)^{′}={e}^{x}-6x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6)-({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n})}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}({x}_{0}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.5}(3.5-1)}{{e}^{3.5}-6} \\\\  & =3.053\\,190…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}(3.053\\,190…-1)}{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}-6} \\\\  & =2.864\\,577…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}(2.864\\,577…-1)}{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,890…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{3}}({x}_{3}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{3}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}(2.833\\,890…-1)}{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,149….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈2.83$ and ${x}_{4}≈2.83$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈2.83,$ rounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "9ac933f2-f01f-51d6-973c-c8ab7550c9d7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.733" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556824} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b467806-b4ba-5d03-bdb3-631ac4ebb45e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.752" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556523} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa0b5de-3e08-5716-8224-20250363178e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83" :question/math-academy-id "q-256862" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{3}$ in the interval $[3,4].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=3.6$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-{x}^{3})^{′}={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2})^{′}={e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =3.6-\\frac{{e}^{3.6}-3(3.6)^{2}}{{e}^{3.6}-6(3.6)} \\\\  & =3.752\\,135…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(3.752\\,135…)-\\frac{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-3(3.752\\,135…)^{2}}{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-6(3.752\\,135…)} \\\\  & =3.733\\,407…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈3.752$ and ${x}_{2}≈3.733,$ rounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "e658d0bc-cfdb-54df-a117-9454d8f9d284" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556582} :answer-field/id #uuid "91adf0bc-26e8-5ef8-a01d-3f8b945f9b47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" :question/math-academy-id "q-256827" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[-2,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2})^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x)^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-8{x}_{n}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-8{x}_{0}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})-8} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-8(2)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)-8} \\\\  & =0.505\\,007…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-8{x}_{1}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})-8} \\\\  & =(0.505\\,007…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)+(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8(0.505\\,007…)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8} \\\\  & ≈0.025,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "f5ab5c9e-f782-5299-aa3a-8aea8958e708" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557341}}] :activity/title "Newton's Method for Optimizing Single-Variable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556422}]}
